<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/intermediate/a-history-of-architectural-styles">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Architectural history is the record of <strong>what each era believed a building should
    be</strong> — permanence, heaven, industry, function, irony, flow. Styles are not fashion
    cycles but worldviews in materials: each one is a bundle of structural technique, social
    ambition and aesthetic theory that answered its century's question. Read this page as an
    engineer reads systems: every style is a kit (an arch here, a frame there), an economy
    (who paid, for what message) and a theory (what a building should admit about itself).
    The kit changes with materials; the theory changes more slowly; and the conversation
    between them — what technology permits versus what culture wants to say — is the engine
    of the whole story.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="classical grammar, the vertical race, the industrial rupture, the modern cascade" widget="equationStep" config={styleCfg} />

  <h2>Classical: proportion as a language (c. 600 bce – present)</h2>
  <p>
    Greece developed the <strong>orders</strong> — Doric, Ionic, Corinthian — which are less
    column styles than proportional systems: a module (the column diameter) scales the
    whole facade, entablature and spacing, so a classical building is arithmetic you can
    see. The Parthenon (447–432 bce) already refines this with optical corrections —
    columns swell (entasis) and lean inward because perfect straightness looks wrong to
    the eye: geometry bent to perception, a very classical move. Rome added the two
    technologies that made space rather than shape: the <strong>arch</strong> (and its
    descendants, the vault and dome), which turns masonry's weakness into a compression
    circuit, and <strong>concrete</strong> with pozzolana. The Pantheon (c. 126 ce) is the
    proof: a 43.3 m dome — still the world's largest unreinforced concrete dome — with
    coffers carving away weight toward the crown and aggregate graded heavier at the
    base: Roman builders doing structural optimisation by recipe. The classical language's
    astonishing durability (Greek temple, Roman basilica, Renaissance palazzo, Georgian
    terrace, the courthouse in your city) rests on what it encodes: proportion, hierarchy,
    permanence — a visual constitution for public life, revivable at will.
  </p>

  <h2>Gothic: the engineering of light (c. 1140–1500)</h2>
  <p>
    The Romanesque church was a fortress of light: barrel vaults push outward along their
    entire length, so the walls must be massive to stay compressed. The Gothic kit
    redirected those forces. <strong>Pointed arches</strong> (steepen a semicircle's thrust
    toward the vertical), <strong>ribbed vaults</strong> (concentrate the load onto ribs
    and piers instead of whole walls), and <strong>flying buttresses</strong> (catch the
    outward thrust outside the wall and deliver it to the ground) turned the cathedral
    into a stone skeleton with a glass infill. The consequence was a structural surplus
    spent on theology: walls dissolved into stained glass, and the vertical race ran to
    Amiens' 42.3 m nave and Beauvais' 48.5 m — until Beauvais' choir vaults fell in 1284,
    the era's honest statement of its factor of safety. Chartres (c. 1194–1220) and its
    peers were cathedrals <em>and</em> construction projects with masonic geometry; treat
    them as the first high-technology buildings — and note that the structural systems
    lesson of this atlas is, in a real sense, their descendant.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>Renaissance</strong> looked back to move forward: Brunelleschi's Florence
    dome (1420–1436, ~45 m span, built without centering by a herringbone brick course the
    ancient Romans would have recognised and the classical orders revived by Alberti and
    codified by Palladio made antiquity a working method rather than a ruin. The
    <strong>Baroque</strong> then weaponised the vocabulary for drama — Bernini's
    colonnade at St Peter's as the church's embracing arms, ceilings dissolving into
    heaven, movement and illusion as explicit goals: architecture as rhetoric. (The
    art-history lesson takes these two in depth; here, note their structural innocence —
    by now the classical kit was syntax available for any meaning.)
  </p>

  <h2>The industrial rupture (1750–1900)</h2>
  <p>
    The 19th century broke the ancient equation "structure = masonry". Iron first: mills
    and bridges in cast and wrought iron; then the exhibition hall that stated the future
    outright — Paxton's <strong>Crystal Palace</strong> (1851): 564 m long, a standardised
    kit of cast-iron parts and factory glass, assembled in months, faster than a
    cathedral's facade. It was not "architecture" by the academies' definition — and that
    was the point: production had outrun theory. Chicago reassembled the argument
    vertically: <strong>steel frames</strong> (Jenney's Home Insurance Building, 1885) carry
    the loads, <strong>passenger elevators</strong> (Otis safety brake, 1852; first
    installations in the 1870s) make the height usable, and the load-bearing wall dies —
    the facade becomes a curtain, hung. Louis Sullivan drew the aesthetic conclusion
    (<em>form follows function</em>, 1896) and gave the skyscraper its tripartite
    classical body. Two reactions ran alongside: the <strong>Arts &amp; Crafts</strong>
    movement (Morris, against industrial ornament) and <strong>Art Nouveau</strong>
    (Horta, Guimard — the machine age answered with sinuous handcraft, iron made to grow
    like plants). And the century was honestly eclectic — Gothic revival churches,
    neoclassical banks, Indo-Saracenic stations (Victoria Terminus, Bombay) — an empire's
    architecture quoting everything it governed.
  </p>

  <h2>Modernism: the claim of one universal style (c. 1900–1970)</h2>
  <p>
    The moderns proposed the first architecture since the classical that claimed to be
    <em>for everyone, everywhere</em>. The Bauhaus (Gropius, Weimar, 1919) fused art with
    industrial training; Le Corbusier issued the programme (<em>Vers une architecture</em>,
    1923: "a house is a machine for living in", and the 1927 Five Points — pilotis, free
    plan, free facade, ribbon windows, roof garden) that the steel-concrete frame finally
    made literal; Mies reduced it to its quietest sentence — "less is more" — in
    steel-and-glass monuments like the Seagram Building (1958); Frank Lloyd Wright ran
    the countercurrent, an organic modernism rooted in prairie and landscape. Behind all
    of them stands the Crystal Palace's kit: frame, curtain wall, standard parts — now
    with a social theory attached (housing the masses, light and air for tenement
    populations). Its failures were real (Corbusier's radiantly rational housing blocks
    often met people nobody interviewed), its legacy unavoidable: every glass tower and
    every concrete frame office is its descendant.
  </p>
  <p>
    Postwar cascades followed fast. <strong>Brutalism</strong> (from the French
    <em>béton brut</em> — raw concrete: Smithsons, Le Corbusier's Marseille block)
    displayed structure and material honestly at civic scale, and is now both despised
    and campaigned for. <strong>High-tech</strong> wore the services on the outside
    (Piano &amp; Rogers' Pompidou Centre, 1977 — escalators as facade): the building as
    honest machine. <strong>Postmodernism</strong> answered modernism's universalism with
    historicism and irony (Venturi: "Less is a bore"; Johnson's Chippendale-topped
    AT&amp;T tower, 1984) — architecture permitted to quote again.
    <strong>Deconstructivism</strong> (the 1988 MoMA show; Gehry's Bilbao Guggenheim,
    1997) tilted planes and warped form with the new structural software; and the
    <strong>parametric present</strong> (Zaha Hadid's curves computed, not modelled)
    makes form-follows-algorithm the latest — not final — answer.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond the Western canon — and beside it</h2>
  <p>
    A style history that stops at Europe is half a subject. <strong>Islamic
    architecture</strong> carried its own kit for a millennium: the horseshoe and pointed
    arch, muqarnas vaulting, geometry-and-calligraphy ornament (figural imagery reserved),
    and the courtyard mosque from Kairouan to Isfahan; Mughal India fused Persian,
    Timurid and Hindu craft into symmetric garden-tombs — the Taj Mahal (1632–53) as the
    type. <strong>East Asian timber framing</strong> (bracket sets, dougong, curved
    eaves) built pagodas seven storeys high with joints that flex in earthquakes —
    Hōryū-ji has stood since the 7th century. <strong>Vernacular</strong> everywhere is
    passive design's pre-industrial laboratory: Yazd's wind-catchers, Andalusian
    courtyards, Norwegian sod roofs, Malian adobe mosques (Djenné — the world's largest
    mud building, replastered annually as a civic festival). These are design
    intelligences, not footnotes — several are currently being re-read as climate
    answers, which is history's usual irony: the future keeps borrowing from what the
    canon forgot.
  </p>

  <h2>Why "styles" ended — and what preservation argues</h2>
  <p>
    Modernism's boldest claim was that there should be <em>no period styles</em> — one
    honest, universal method. Postmodernism's counter-claim was that this was itself a
    style, and a historically blind one. Since then the field has largely stopped naming
    movements and started arguing about problems: carbon, retrofit, housing, reuse. That
    shift makes <strong>preservation</strong> the discipline of the moment: the Venice
    Charter (1964) set the ethics — keep the authentic fabric, distinguish
    <em>restoration</em> (return to a documented state) from <em>reconstruction</em>
    (speculative, discouraged), and add new work legibly rather than in pastiche.
    Every retrofit of a brutalist icon or a Georgian terrace is now the live form of
    architectural history: deciding, with the whole record in view, what the next
    generation inherits.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> styles follow technology automatically. Materials
  permit; cultures choose. The steel frame arrived in 1885, yet American clients spent
  fifty more years wrapping it in Gothic and classical clothing because banks and
  courthouses <em>wanted</em> to speak permanence. Technology writes the possible;
  the style debate writes the actual — which is why the history is worth arguing about
  rather than merely memorising.</p>

  <h2>Between Rome and Gothic: Byzantine, Romanesque — and the Islamic conduit</h2>
  <p>
    The ladder has middle rungs worth climbing. <strong>Byzantine</strong> architecture
    solved the dome-on-square problem with <strong>pendentives</strong> (curved triangles
    bridging a round dome to a square bay) — Hagia Sophia (537 ce) floats a 31 m dome
    on a crown of windows so the masonry seems to vanish into light: engineering as
    theology, five centuries before Chartres. <strong>Romanesque</strong> (c. 900–1150)
    rebuilt the West's confidence in vaulted stone — thick walls, round arches,
    fortress proportions, the pilgrimage routes of Santiago de Compostela stitching
    Europe into a construction network. And the period's great transfer ran through
    Islamic lands: pointed arches (structural maths refined in mosques from Cairo to
    Isfahan), ribbed vaults in the mosque architecture of Córdoba and the Maghreb,
    and the optics-and-geometry toolkit of Arabic science — all arriving in Europe
    through Sicily, Spain and the Crusader contact zones. Gothic is best read not as
    a spontaneous miracle but as Europe's synthesis of imported mathematics with
    its own masonic ambition — the "interactions within and across cultures" lens
    of the syllabus, load-bearing in the history itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The historiography problem.</strong> "Style" is itself a 19th-century
    invention (Wölfflin's comparative categories; the grand slide-lecture survey), and
    every periodisation smuggles in a theory — progress (modernists), decline and
    revival (classical authors), or pluralism (today's default). Reading architectural
    history well means reading <em>how</em> the story is built: whose buildings got
    counted (monuments, not houses), whose labour vanished (the unnamed masons behind
    every named architect), and what the canon's silences were until they were
    contested.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Materials as the spine.</strong> Strip the story to its structural skeleton
    and a clean sequence appears: load-bearing stone (arch to distribute, buttress to
    resist) → iron and steel frames (height, curtain walls) → reinforced concrete
    (spans, cantilevers, sculptural freedom) → glass as infill and attitude → engineered
    timber (cross-laminated timber towers now climbing past 80 m) → and the emerging
    chapter: low-carbon materials retrofitting the whole canon. The styles debate runs
    <em>on</em> this spine; when the spine changes — as it is again now, for carbon
    reasons — expect a style argument to follow it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Is the parametric turn a style or a toolset (the
    honest answer: both, in proportion still being argued)? Will carbon accounting do to
    20th-century glass towers what fire codes did to timber frontages — rewrite the
    default building overnight? And can preservation scale to the ordinary (the
    1960s housing estate, the steel-frame high street), where most of the embodied
    carbon and most of the people live? The next chapter of this history is being
    decided in planning committees, not manifestos — which may be the field's most
    significant stylistic break since 1919.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Watkin, A History of Western Architecture; Frampton, Modern Architecture: A Critical History; Trachtenberg &amp; Hyman, Architecture: From Prehistory to Postmodernity; the Venice Charter (1964); the art-history ladder of this atlas.</p>

